probability of road crashes, highlighting the need to study the impact of secondary task performance while driving. A range of methodologies have been employed to accurately assess the magnitude and implications of driver distraction on driving performance. One commonly used approach involves the analysis of eyetracking data and gaze patterns to gauge the impact of distractions on drivers (4). Additionally, physiological indicators, such as heart rate variability and electroencephalography (EEG) signals, serve as valuable metrics for measuring the cognitive workload and stress levels induced by distractions during driving (5). Devices such as cameras, sensors, and data loggers also offer an alternative means of capturing driver distraction activities (6). Furthermore, self-reported questionnaires have proven effective in elucidating drivers’ subjective perceptions of situations involving distracted driving (7). These multidimensional measurement techniques offer a thorough framework for evaluating the effects of driver distraction on road safety. Driver distraction–based road crashes may be reduced, if not eliminated, with the advancements in automation in the automotive sector. Advanced driver assistance systems (ADAS) and autonomous vehicles (AV) are examples of automated driving systems that are effective in reducing human error, improving vehicle control, and enhancing road safety (8, 9). Although the advent of fully autonomous vehicles in the traffic stream might theoretically eliminate road crashes caused by human error, this is far from reality because of the relatively high cost of AVs and inadequate infrastructure in developing economies such as India. The successful implementation of any new technology-based solution relies greatly on public perception and opinion. Therefore, it is essential to carefully consider this aspect before integrating any solution into existing systems. Questionnaire surveys are regarded as highly effective practical tools given their ability to collect data from a large sample of respondents. The responses obtained can be used further for detailed analysis, providing insights into how road users perceive driver distractions (7). Although capable of yielding substantial responses, questionnaire surveys are inadequate substitutes for field operation tests (FOT) when conducting comprehensive analyses of driver behavior in real-world driving scenarios. FOTs employ in-vehicle data collection devices to monitor various driver and vehicle-related parameters. Although driving simulators are frequently used in modern research to simulate real-world driving situations (10–13), they fall short of replicating the complexities of real road conditions. Consequently, conducting studies on real roads becomes essential. However, certain critical
distractions, such as cell phone usage while driving, present substantial risks in real traffic conditions. To address this, carefully designed controlled road environments that mirror realistic road geometry and traffic scenarios offer a reliable platform for investigating the effects of distractions on driver performance and safety. This study is one such attempt to understand driving behavior in a realistic road environment. Through this study, we aim to improve the overall understanding of driver distraction and its underlying causes, with a focus on examining the impacts of texting while driving using the FOT approach.
Literature Review
Performing secondary tasks such as phone calls and texting while driving has been found to significantly impair driver performance, resulting in reduced speeds, increased reaction times, loss of control of the vehicle, and increased road crashes. Researchers in the past have investigated the impact of these secondary tasks while driving using various approaches, as outlined in the following sections.
Surveys
Foreman et al. conducted a discrete choice experiment to assess texting while driving, with 345 adult participants in the study. The participants were given scenarios and asked if they would text and drive in specific circumstances. According to the findings, individuals were more inclined to text when driving on rural roads, and if the person receiving the message was considered important by the driver (14). Moore and Brown assessed the relationship between self-regulation, habitual text messaging, trait mindfulness, and texting while driving. An online survey was incorporated to collect data from 170 respondents. The results showed that habitual texting behavior was significantly associated with higher frequencies of texting while driving. The findings suggested that the drivers texted while driving out of pure habit, which was influenced by deficiencies in self-regulation (15). Hill et al. conducted an online survey–based investigation to examine the attitudes of college students toward distracted driving. The survey attracted 4,964 responses, with the age of the respondents being 21.82 6 2.95 (mean 6 SD) years. The findings demonstrated that around 90% of the students used mobile phones for phone calls and texting while driving. Moreover, the students expressed the view that stringent regulations on mobile phone usage while driving would influence their behavior (16). Mikoski et al., in their study involving 492 participants aged from 18 to 76 years, examined drivers’
