assessments of distraction risk and other driving scenarios. The study revealed a pronounced inclination toward phone use while driving among young drivers compared with their older counterparts. Moreover, the drivers reported feeling more at ease while driving under daylight conditions than when driving at night (7). In an online survey–based study conducted by Oscar Oviedo involving 484 participants, it was found that experienced drivers who considered texting while driving to be a high-risk task and those who supported the concept of reducing driving speeds during phone usage displayed a decreased tendency to engage in texting while driving (17).
Naturalistic Driving Studies (NDS)
Morgenstern et al. investigated whether drivers reduce their speeds while texting and driving on highways. A 134-sample naturalistic driving data set was considered to assess the speeds of the drivers on highways. The results indicated that drivers reduced their speeds by more than 2 km/h while texting and regained their initial speeds after the texting event (18). Schneidereit et al. conducted a naturalistic driving–based study with 131 participant drivers aged from 16 to 74 years. The study’s objective was to examine the driving speeds before and after performing secondary tasks, which included texting, eating, and radio adjustment. The analysis revealed the tendency of the drivers to alter their speeds, particularly when driving at high speeds and engaging in secondary tasks (19). Furthermore, Atwood et al. (20) observed that the texting rate while driving had a significant variation based on age, where drivers aged from 16 to 19 years exhibited a texting rate four times greater than the drivers aged from 30 to 64 years. Wang et al. investigated the effect of phone usage on driving performance, focusing on parameters such as speed, lane offset, and headway. The study involved 51 participant drivers aged from 25 to 59 years. The findings demonstrated a significant reduction in the standard deviations of the speed, lane offset, and headway when drivers engaged in mobile phones under naturalistic driving conditions (21). Bastos et al. examined mobile phone usage while driving, involving six participant drivers with the age range from 19 to 38 years. The study revealed prolonged mobile phone use among drivers during longer trips and at lower driving speeds (22). Christoph et al. examined the interaction times between drivers, mobile phones, and GPS devices. The study comprised 21 drivers with a mean age of 37.3 6 9.9 (mean 6 SD) years and driving experience of over 10,000 km per year. Results indicated that the drivers spent around 1% and 4% of their driving time engaging with GPS and mobile phones, respectively.
Additionally, it was observed that the drivers maintained their driving speeds while performing visual-manual tasks (23). Simons-Morton et al. examined the visual distraction of drivers away from the road when performing secondary tasks while driving. Forty-two young drivers aged 16.4 6 0.22 (mean 6 SD) years and holding a driving license for less than three weeks participated in the study. It was observed that longer glances away from the road increased the likelihood of a road crash and nearcrash events (24).
Driving Simulator Studies
Driving simulators have been used extensively to study the effect of in-vehicle distractions on driving behavior. Choudhary and Velaga conducted a driving simulatorbased study where the authors investigated and modeled the impacts of conversation and texting on the driving performance of Indian drivers in mean speed and crashavoiding abilities. The results showed that the drivers compensated for the additional workload by reducing their mean speeds by 5.29 m/s during the texting task. Also, phone calls while driving were found to be more dangerous for young drivers because of their risk-taking behavior and lack of experience (10, 25). Yannis et al. investigated the impact of texting on the behavior and safety of teenage drivers on urban and rural roads. The study included 34 drivers aged from 18 to 28 years. The impact of texting while driving was studied, considering parameters such as traffic environment, weather conditions, driver characteristics, self-reported driving behavior, and road crash occurrence. The findings from their study revealed that texting while driving significantly increased the occurrence of road crashes, with a higher likelihood of occurrence of road crashes in urban regions than in rural areas (26). Peng et al. conducted a study on 28 drivers across four age groups, aiming to assess the effect of entering and reading text on in-vehicle information systems (IVIS) while driving. The authors observed that even short text entry tasks could significantly degrade the driver’s longitudinal and lateral control, suggesting that time taken for the text entry or reading tasks significantly affected the distraction levels (27). In a study by Drews et al., 40 young drivers aged from 19 to 23 years participated in a high-fidelity driving simulator–based study, performing a single task (driving) and a dual task (driving and text messaging). Findings showed that the participants in the dual-task condition responded to the onset of braking lights with delay and displayed impairments in forward and lateral control compared with participants in the driving-only condition. Additionally, it was observed that drivers who texted while driving had a higher crash rate than non-texting drivers (28). Bendak, in their simulator-based study, assessed the effects of
