Field Tests
Field tests on closed controlled tracks have also been used in the past to examine the effects of texting while driving. These studies are safe and effective in simulating distraction-inducing tasks. In a closed-track field experiment conducted by Yager et al., it was observed that texting while driving significantly affected response time and lane deviation. Also, Text message reading and writing rates were much lower while driving than in the office (30). Yager, in another study, examined the impact of the type of text entry, considering manual texting, using Siri, and using Vlingo. It was observed that driver response time was significantly affected by all three text entry methods, and the drivers’ feedback revealed that they felt less safe while texting and driving compared with the baseline conditions (31). Owens et al. (32) conducted a field experiment with 20 participant drivers and found that handheld texting while driving induced a high mental workload and significantly affected the visual attention of drivers. Neurauter et al., found that middle-aged drivers had higher lane deviation and speed variance while texting compared with the younger and older drivers. The texting task also significantly increased the drivers’ eyesoff-road time compared with the speech-based tasks (33). Table 1 summarizes the existing research on the effect of secondary tasks on driver performance considering the methodologies of investigation discussed above. Eye-tracker technology plays a crucial role in driver distraction investigations as it enables the observation and analysis of eye movements while engaging in secondary tasks. These devices record and monitor the driver’s gaze behavior, providing valuable information about the driver’s attention allocation on the road. Najar et al. conducted a study to examine how in-vehicle objects and spatial distractions affect driver gaze behavior and performance, using an FOT. Their research revealed that object-based distraction led to decreased fixation durations and fixation counts on the area of interest (AOI), along with a significant increase in driving errors (4). In another eye-tracking-based study involving 30 drivers, Desmet et al. found that performing a secondary task, such as a phone call while driving, led to fewer fixations on the road signs, vehicles, and the speedometer. Additionally, the visual scan pattern displayed a wider
spatial distribution during the phone call compared with the no-phone call condition (43). Similarly, Hashash et al., in their study with 26 university students, found that using mobile phones for texting and social media led to decreased visual attention to the road, with the texting task exhibiting a more severe impact on driver performance (44). Therefore, eye tracking presents itself as an effective method for quantifying driver distraction levels during secondary task engagement. As discussed above, numerous studies in the past have explored the impact of mobile phone usage such as texting and phone calls while driving, the majority of which have been conducted using driving simulators. The use of driving simulators offers distinct advantages, such as examination of the driver’s behavior across diverse driving scenarios that are difficult to replicate in real-world contexts. This is particularly advantageous in scenarios that involve potential injury, such as the sudden appearance of obstacles on the road. Although driving simulators offer controlled environments and the ability to simulate diverse scenarios, they lack the complexities and uncertainties of real-world driving that field studies can address effectively. Furthermore, considering that the drivers participating in simulator-based studies realize the virtual set-up, they may not engage with the driving scenarios as seriously as they would when driving in real life, even in the absence of traffic. Researchers have turned to naturalistic driving studies (NDS) to overcome the virtual set-up shortcomings of driving simulator–based studies, as an NDS provides authentic road conditions for investigating driver behavior. An NDS enables drivers to navigate freely, and the data recording devices installed in the vehicles provide the researchers with real-world driving data. Despite their potential benefits, NDS’s are characterized by high costs and time demands and are ethically sensitive, offering limited control to the researchers. Moreover, identifying the precise cause of a driver’s visual distraction such as traffic signals, pedestrians, mobile phones, animals, passengers, and so forth would require integrating eye-tracking technology into the NDS. Performing field operations tests in controlled road environments ensures precise testing conditions, allowing for the safe performance of secondary tasks while driving. Despite its advantage of real-world testing, this approach has seen limited adoption in the past for studying driver behavior given the high costs, logistical challenges, and the inability to simulate diverse risky driving scenarios as effectively as driving simulators. Considering the advantages and drawbacks outlined for these types of study, this current study adopted an FOT-based approach in the absence of traffic, considering the available resources. Additionally, incorporating an eye-tracker device during the study facilitated the
