examination of the cause of visual distraction. This approach would offer several advantages: a) enhanced control over the desired driving scenarios under examination; b) significant cost efficiency; c) ethical alignment; and d) mitigation of possible hazardous conditions that could compromise safety. The deliberate exclusion of road traffic from the study was justified, considering that texting while driving in real-world traffic, especially in countries such as India characterized by mixed traffic conditions, would involve high complexity and extreme risk. Moreover, the absence of prior research that investigated the pure distraction potential of texting while driving under realistic road and weather conditions in the absence of traffic highlights the novelty of this study. Consequently, the current research displays the potential to serve as a pilot investigation in this domain, offering valuable insights into the sole distraction potential of texting while driving. The principal objective of this research is to assess the exclusive impact of texting on driver performance under a realistic road environment, focusing on quantifiable metrics, which include speed, acceleration, and gaze parameters such as fixation count and duration.
Methodology
Participants
Convenience and snowball sampling techniques were incorporated to recruit participants for the study. The majority of the participants were students and the working staff of the Indian Institute of Technology Hyderabad. Participation in the study was voluntary, and the participants could withdraw from the study at any stage. The advertisement for participation in the study was circulated by e-mail. The shortlisting criteria for participation in the study included possession of a valid Indian driving license (manual transmission car) and a minimum of one year of driving experience in real-world traffic conditions. Thirtythree participants voluntarily participated in the study. Data from two participants were considered invalid because of poor instrument calibration and data not being recorded appropriately. The final study sample comprised of 31 drivers (29 males, 2 females) aged from 19 to 42 years (M = 25.54, SD = 4.08), with a mean driving experience of 4.93 years. The participants received incentives in the form of gifts as a token of appreciation at the end of the study. The purpose of the study was clearly expressed to the participants, and the participants signed a written consent form before the start of the study.
Instrumentation
The FOT was conducted using an instrumented passenger car equipped with four video cameras, a GPS device,
ADAS, and a Vbox pro data logger (Figure 1). Also, the drivers wore the Pupil’s invisible eye-tracker glasses during the study drive. The GPS device was mounted on top of the car and was used to get the vehicle’s coordinates during the study. The four video cameras were set up inside the car to give a clear view of the outer road environment and the driver. The data logger was used to record the kinematic parameters, such as the speed and acceleration of the car during the drive. Pupil’s invisible eye-tracker glasses were used to record the gaze movement of the driver during the study (Figure 2) (45).
Test Route
Since the test involved driving under distracted conditions, a controlled road environment set-up was used for the study. The Technology Innovation Hub on Autonomous Navigation and Data Acquisition Systems (TiHAN) connected autonomous vehicles (CAV) testbed facility at the Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Hyderabad was used as a controlled road environment for the study. The testbed allowed for the testing of critical scenarios that could occur in real traffic conditions. The testbed facility was equipped with varying road geometry, including multilane roads, curved road sections, S-curves, and intersections. Also, traffic control devices such as traffic signs, signals, and road markings were available at the testbed for efficient use. The total road network at the testbed facility was approximately 1 km in length (Figure 3).
Procedure
Figure 4 depicts the brief experimental procedure considered in the study. A detailed briefing was given to the participants before the start of the study to familiarize them with the test conditions. Calibration of the eyetracker device was done before the trial drive. Also, adjustments for powered lenses were considered if the participant wore eyeglasses with powered lenses. Before beginning the study drive, the participants drove on the testbed to get accustomed to the test route and the instrumentation inside the vehicle. The driver’s seat and mirrors were adjusted to fit each driver’s comfort. All participants signed a written consent form with details related to the conduct of the study, risks involved, benefits, data security, and so forth. Once the participants felt confident and comfortable in using the instrumented test vehicle for the study, they were taken to the starting point of the test route at the testbed. During the study drive, the participants drove twice on the defined path. First under the baseline condition (no distraction) and the second time under the text messaging condition. During the second trip, the participants
