drove while being engaged in reading and replying to text messages as the car was in motion. The text messages included questions such as ‘‘What is your favorite food dish?’’ ‘‘Write down the title of your favorite song.’’, and ‘‘What is your favorite color?’’ The participants were instructed not to reply to the text messages when they stopped at any intersection during a red-light signal. A Samsung M21 2021 edition smartphone was used to complete the texting task, with a QWERTY keyboard setting and type/swipe options enabled. A post-experiment questionnaire was shared with the participants after completing the driving-based study. The questionnaire comprised various sections for providing feedback on the instrumentation and collecting participant demographic characteristics. A view of the FOT conducted during the study is shown in Figure 5.
Results
A significance level (a) of 0.05 was considered for statistical inference throughout this study. The effect of texting on driving performance was studied by considering the following parameters: fixation count, fixation duration, mean speed, longitudinal acceleration, and lateral acceleration. Driving experience and age were considered as classification parameters for the driving participants to analyze if age and driving experience influenced driving performance in a distracted state caused by the texting task. The participant’s age and driving experience data were collected through the post-experiment questionnaire. Based on age, the drivers were classified into two age groups: a) less than or equal to 25 years, and b)
more than 25 years. Also, based on their driving experience, the drivers were classified into three groups: a) less than 2 years, b) 2 to 5 years, and c) more than 5 years. The data sets considered for analysis were assessed for parametric properties, such as meeting the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance, which are necessary for parametric statistical analysis. If the data were found to satisfy these assumptions, parametric statistical tests were employed; otherwise, non-parametric statistical tests were used for analysis. In the rest of this paper, ‘‘parametric in nature’’ refers to data sets that satisfied the parametric assumptions, whereas ‘‘non-parametric in nature’’ indicates data sets that did not meet these assumptions.
Gaze Behavior
In the study, gaze was defined as the driver’s point of observation inside and outside the car. Meanwhile, fixation was represented as the eye movement fixed at any object of interest. The gaze behavior of the driver was analyzed for the fixation count and fixation duration on the AOI considered in the study. Fixation count denoted the number of fixations on the AOI, whereas fixation duration denoted the time duration for which the eye remained fixated on the AOI. iMotions software was used to analyze the gaze-related data obtained from the Pupil’s invisible eye-tracker glasses. As shown in Figure 6, the AOIs considered were the roadway in front and the mobile phone used for text messaging during the study. The fixation count and duration on the roadway under the baseline and texting conditions were compared and tested for significant differences.
