Fixation Count
The observations from the analysis highlighted the critical distraction potential of texting as a secondary task, as the mean fixation count on the road was reduced by 44.37% when the participants texted while driving. The reduction in the fixation count on the AOI (roadway) during an in-vehicle distraction task was consistent with the observations recorded by Najar et al. (4). When different driving experience groups were considered for analysis, it was observed that the reduction in mean fixation count on the road was highest for ‘‘less than 2 years’’ group, followed by ‘‘2 to 5 years’’ and ‘‘more than 5 years.’’ This observation indicated that the more experienced drivers would get distracted to a lesser extent during the texting operation, as shown by the mean fixation count. While comparing the age groups for distraction extent, the study revealed that the drivers from the ‘‘more than 25 years’’ age group were distracted to a greater extent as compared with the ‘‘less than or equal to 25 years’’ age group, indicating that the distraction levels were proportional to driver age. A possible explanation for this observation could be the early exposure of the younger age group to mobile phones and other high-end technologies compared with older individuals. Early exposure to mobile phones might have led to habituated mobile phone use while performing other primary tasks.
Fixation Duration
The analysis revealed that the drivers’ mean fixation durations were not significantly affected by changes in driving conditions (baseline and texting conditions). These findings contradicted the observations of the distraction-based study conducted by Najar et al. (4). Classifications of drivers based on driving experience and age also did not bring out any significant difference. A few probable reasons for this could be the habituation of the driver to texting while driving, driving experience under similar conditions, and the relatively small sample size of participants in the study that could not yield significant differences in the mean fixation duration values. Based on the study’s observations, it was inferred that the mean fixation durations of the drivers could be independent of driving conditions, age, and driving experience.
Mean Speed
The results from the study showed that the mean speeds on the road were reduced by 34% (6.82 km/h) when the participants drove under the texting condition. The significant reduction in speeds during the texting task aligned with the observations from previous studies (10,
12, 18). When different driving experience groups were considered for analysis, it was observed that the reduction in mean speed was the highest for the ‘‘more than 5 years’’ group, followed by the ‘‘2 to 5 years’’ and ‘‘less than 2 years’’ groups. This indicated that the driving experience was proportional to the reduction in mean speeds of drivers under texting conditions and that the more experienced drivers tended to prioritize safety. While comparing the age groups for significant changes in mean speed, it was observed that the ‘‘more than 25 years’’ age group had a higher speed differential than the ‘‘less than or equal to 25 years’’ age group. This observation indicated that drivers with greater driving experience and older age prioritized safety and therefore significantly reduced their mean speeds while texting and driving.
Longitudinal Acceleration
It was observed that the mean longitudinal acceleration of drivers was not significantly affected by changes in driving conditions (baseline and texting conditions). Classifications of drivers based on driving experience and age also did not bring out any significant change in longitudinal acceleration values. These observations suggested that longitudinal acceleration may have been among the factors independent of driving conditions and notably dependent on the individual driving behavior.
Lateral Acceleration
The results revealed that the mean lateral acceleration was reduced by 46.72% when the participants drove under texting conditions. The decreased lateral acceleration during the texting condition would result in the reduced ability of the driver to change direction or avoid obstacles swiftly. Moreover, when encountering a sudden obstacle on the road, the driver may oversteer, resulting in overcompensation and potentially destabilizing the vehicle’s trajectory, thus hampering safety. When different driving experience groups were considered for analysis, a significant difference in mean lateral acceleration was only observed for the ‘‘less than 2 years’’ group. These results align with previous studies, which have shown that greater driving experience leads to less variation in lateral acceleration under distracted driving conditions (25). While comparing the age groups for significant changes in mean lateral acceleration, it was observed that the ‘‘more than 25 years’’ age group had a higher difference in mean lateral acceleration than the ‘‘less than or equal to 25 years’’ age group. The significant difference in lateral acceleration under distracted driving conditions, with a more pronounced effect in older age groups, aligned with the findings reported in
1792 Transportation Research Record 2679(2)


previous studies (46, 47). The findings of this study highlighted that drivers with less driving experience and higher age were more vulnerable to loss of maneuverability when texting while driving.
Effect Size (Cohen’s d)
The results from the analysis revealed that fixation count, mean speed, and lateral acceleration were significantly affected by the distracted condition caused by texting while driving. The Cohen’s d values observed were 1.219, 1.782, and 1.109 for the fixation count, mean speed, and lateral acceleration, respectively. These values indicated that the mean speed of the driver was most significantly affected, followed by fixation count, and lateral acceleration.
Applications
Texting while driving has the potential to divert the driver’s cognitive attention away from the primary task of driving, which highlights the importance of assessing the impact of texting on driver behavior to ensure the safety of all road users. The study was based on an FOT conducted in a controlled road environment, with 31 drivers taking part in the experiment. The study examined the gaze and kinematic parameters to assess the impact of texting on driving behavior. Despite the limitation of the sample size of participants in the study, the results indicated a significant effect of texting on driver behavior. The findings from the study and their possible applications have been outlined below,
Gaze Behavior. The fixation count and duration were used to gauge the visual attention of the driver on the road. The results revealed that texting led to a significant reduction in the fixation count on the road. These findings highlight the risk associated with texting while driving, as eyes off the road is one of the major causes of road crashes. These insights could be used in public safety campaigns and driver education programs to emphasize the importance of visual attention on the road. Also, automotive manufacturers could consider using the findings from the study to develop ADAS that monitor drivers, give warning alerts, and apply corrective measures when visual attention is compromised.
Kinematic Parameters. Speed, longitudinal acceleration, and lateral acceleration were considered as kinematic parameters to assess driver performance during the study. The results showed that texting while driving had a significant impact on the speed and lateral acceleration of the drivers. These findings highlight that texting while driving compromised vehicle control and stability. These
findings could be used by policymakers and government agencies to strengthen laws on mobile phone usage while driving, particularly texting.
Effect of Age and Driving Experience. It was observed that drivers from the less driving experience and higher age groups showed significant differences in the lateral acceleration and fixation count while texting and driving compared with baseline driving conditions. Higher age and greater driving experience groups showed a significantly greater reduction in speeds during the texting condition. This could be considered as a cautious and safety-positive behavior, as lowering speeds while being distracted would lessen the impact of any potential crash. Although the lowering of speeds would, in one way, mitigate the impact of a potential crash, it would still compromise traffic flow and efficiency. Therefore, although experienced drivers perform better under distraction than less experienced ones, texting while driving cannot be justified for any level of experience or age group of drivers. These insights from the study could be used by insurance companies in adjusting premiums based on age and driving experience, considering the risks involved resulting from mobile phone usage while driving.
Overall Impact. Overall, the findings from the study offer valuable insights for policymakers, educational campaigns, industry stakeholders, and government agencies involved in addressing distraction resulting from mobile phone usage while driving, given the profound impact of texting on driver behavior. Notably, the observed decline in visual attention through this study, evidenced by a nearly 45% reduction in fixation count on the roadway, poses significant safety risks. Furthermore, the varying effect of age and driving experience on driver behavior in texting-induced distracted scenarios highlights the importance of tailored regulatory measures. Policymakers and government agencies could thus consider implementing specific guidelines and regulations for different age and experience groups of drivers, thereby enhancing overall traffic efficiency and road safety.
Limitations and Future Scope
Some of the limitations of the current study, which form a base for future studies, are as follows,
There was no traffic on the roads during the study. As the current study focused on identifying the distraction caused purely by texting conditions, in the absence of any other distraction caused by other potential distraction elements such as other vehicles, pedestrians, or any other object on the roadway.
Singh et al 1793


The current study only considered text messaging as a distraction-inducing task. Future research could include other secondary tasks while driving such as phone calls, reaching for objects, eating/ drinking, driver–passenger conversations, and so forth. The sample size considered during the study was relatively small, which could have influenced the outcomes of the results and findings from the study. Also, the age group and gender ratio considered during the study could be improved, as considerably younger participants took part as drivers, with 29 male participants and only two female. Future studies may consider a larger sample size, varied ages, and a balanced gender ratio.
Conclusion
This study was conceptualized to investigate the effects of texting as a secondary task on driving behavior, measured in gaze parameters (fixation count and duration) and vehicle kinematics (speed, longitudinal acceleration, and lateral acceleration). The results found that texting while driving significantly affected the mean speed, lateral acceleration, and fixation count on the roadway. Cohen’s d values revealed that the texting task had a maximum effect on the mean speed, followed by the fixation count and lateral acceleration. The independent variables considered for the study, such as age and driving experience, significantly affected the driver’s behavior. When fixation count was considered, it was observed that higher driving experience led to lower distraction levels, whereas the distraction levels increased with age. When mean speed was considered, it was observed that drivers from the greater experience and older age groups considered in the study reduced their driving speeds to a greater extent as compared with drivers from the less experienced and younger age groups. When considering the lateral acceleration, it was observed that drivers from higher age groups had a greater difference in lateral acceleration than drivers of younger age groups. The study attempted to demonstrate the adverse consequences of text messaging while driving using an FOT approach, as previous studies based on driving simulators suggest that the use of real road driving conditions could yield more precise inferences on the impact of texting while driving (25, 37, 41). As the current study did not consider the road crash risk scenarios, the use of an ‘‘FOT approach in a naturalistic road set-up’’ over a driving simulator may be justified. Also, the findings emphasize the necessity for stringent rules against texting while driving because of the significant safety hazard that it presents to the safety of the driver and other road users.
